{"id":"nursing-home-low-staff-related","title":"Nursing homes below the State middle on staffing and high on related-company balances","question":"Which homes report fewer nurse hours than the middle California home while most of what they own is a balance owed by related companies, and what has the State cited them for?","finding":"22 Bay Area homes are on both lists: below the California middle of 4.25 nurse hours per resident day, with half or more of their assets owed to them by related parties ($352.9 million between them, and $85.1 million of net income in their latest year). The State issued them 41 citations or penalties from 2019 to June 2024, 16 of them for nursing hours. Lowest on staffing: Marin Post Acute, San Rafael, at 3.69 hours with $18.9 million owed to it.","how":"Homes from the related-balance screen (net amount owed by related parties at least half of total assets and at least $3 million in the latest State report) whose nurse hours per resident day in the federal daily payroll file are below the California middle. Citations and penalties are State enforcement actions issued from January 2019, matched by State facility number through the State crosswalk: all actions, those of class AA or A, the nursing-hours administrative penalties, the final amount due, and actions the State marks as related to a death.","limits":"Two patterns side by side are not a link between them. A home can staff near the legal minimum and be well run; a balance held by a chain is not proof of loss: the chain may be paying that home's bills from it. Half of all homes are below the middle by definition. The State citation file ends in June 2024, lists actions still open to appeal, and matches by facility number, so a home that changed its licence may show fewer actions than it had. Federal fines and inspection findings for single homes are not on disk (the federal catalogue refused automated access). What would settle the question is the home's audited statements and its r